WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.110
Marine salt deposits form when evaporation concentrates dissolved substances enough for

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.510
minerals to precipitate. Halite is sodium chloride;

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.730
gypsum and other soluble minerals can occur with it.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.590
Warm, dry conditions help, but the basin also needs restricted exchange.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:22.270
Open-ocean circulation can mix the concentrated brine away, preventing a thick

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.030
mineral sequence from accumulating.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.130
Narrow young seas, coastal lagoons and tidal flats can provide suitable

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.850
settings. A low barrier limits exchange.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:36.950
Seawater or runoff can replace water lost to evaporation, allowing repeated

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.010
mineral accumulation. If the basin becomes more isolated, trapped water can

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.910
evaporate further. Repeated replenishment helps explain why a substantial salt sequence

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.730
need not represent the evaporation of one small original puddle.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.800
After burial, salt is weak and less dense than much of

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.240
the overlying rock. Stress can make it flow as a solid.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.520
Salt moving upward can bend or pierce sedimentary layers and form

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.980
a salt dome. The moving yellow material is not magma:

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.760
deposition happened first, then burial and solid-state deformation changed the original

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:10.480
layered geometry.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.140
A weak salt layer can let overlying rocks slide, fold and

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.880
fault, as in the Jura example.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.620
Where salt reaches the surface, gravity can spread it downslope as

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.840
a salt glacier. A dome, a weak detachment layer and a

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.140
salt glacier therefore share material behavior but have different geometries and

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.000
settings. Look at the whole structure before choosing its name.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.760
The Gulf Coast figure links Mesozoic restricted basins, thick salt deposition,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.060
later sediment burial and salt movement.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.980
Rising and spreading salt creates domes, folds, faults and irregular seafloor

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.700
relief. The Sigsbee Escarpment marks the front of a large subsurface

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.040
salt mass in this model.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.120
Some associated structures trap oil and gas;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.300
the salt does not manufacture petroleum simply by rising.
